tive is clearly worse.","points":["The knight forks the rook and the pawn: both can't be saved at once.","Moves the attacked knight to safety.","The next best move, 27.Na3, would drop White's winning chances from 50% to 39% (−1.26).","27.Nd6 forks the rook and the pawn.","Black answers 27…Nxd5, taking the bishop on d5, leaving White with an isolated pawn on b2 and a king with pawns missing from its shelter.","Engine line: 27…Nxd5 28.cxd5 Rc2 29.f4 exf4 30.Rxd4 Kc7 (0.00)."]},{"ply":56,"name":"28…Rc2","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"28…Rc2! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The rook reaches the seventh rank, where 2 pawns sit and the king is cut off on the back rank.","Attacks the b2 pawn, which is undefended.","The next best move, 28…Kc7, would drop Black's winning chances from 50% to 35% (+1.67).","28…Rc2 wins a pawn, picking it up with 29…exf4, and gets the rook out of the way of Nxc8.","Sets up a sequence and wins a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 29…exf4.","White was threatening Nxc8, winning the exchange. The rook steps away."]},{"ply":62,"name":"31…Rxc1","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"31…Rxc1! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the rook on c1.","The next best move, 31…d3, would drop Black's winning chances from 48% to 31% (+2.21).","31…Rxc1 trades off the attacked rook instead of losing it to Rxc2+.","White was threatening Rxc2+, winning a rook. The rook takes something with it instead.","Trades rooks.","The rook on c1 can be taken by the rook on e1."]},{"ply":95,"name":"48.hxg5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"48.hxg5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the pawn on g5.","The next best move, 48.Ne6+, would drop White's winning chances from 59% to 44% (−0.61).","48.hxg5 wins a pawn and threatens gxf6, winning two pawns.","Takes the g5 pawn and wins a pawn once the captures settle.","It threatens gxf6, winning two pawns, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black was threatening …gxh4, winning a pawn. hxg5 takes care of it."]},{"ply":99,"name":"50.gxf6","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"50.gxf6 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 50% to 7%.","points":["Black answers 50…Rg3: the rook joins the attack: it now hits 3 squares around the White king.","After Rg3, Black wins 4 points of material within 5 moves.","Better was 50.Rh1 (0.00): the rook takes the open h-file."],"better":"50.Rh1 takes the open h-file. Engine line: 50…Rc8 51.Rh3 Bf1 52.Rg3 Be2 53.gxf6 Rh8 54.Nxd4 exd4 55.Kxd4 Bd1 (0.00)."},{"ply":100,"name":"50…Rg3","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"50…Rg3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The rook joins the attack: it now hits 3 squares around the White king.","Attacks the b3 pawn, which is undefended.","The next best move, 50…Rg4+, would drop Black's winning chances from 93% to 10% (+6.10).","50…Rg3 wins a rook for a pawn, picking it up with 51…Rxc3, and threatens …Re3#, with mate in 1.","Sets up a sequence and wins a rook for a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 51…Rxc3.","It threatens …Re3#, with mate in 1, so White has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":101,"name":"51.Rc6+","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"51.Rc6+ is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 5% to 0%. It allows mate in 2.","points":["Black answers 51…Bxc6, which takes the rook on c6.","After Bxc6, Black wins 2 points of material within 3 moves.","Better was 51.Rc3 (−8.20), which protects the b3 pawn, which was hanging."],"better":"51.Rc3 threatens Rxg3, winning a rook and a bishop, and stops the mate threat …Re3#. After 51…Rxc3 52.Nxd4 exd4 53.f7 Rc8 54.Kxd4 Rf8, White is 6 points of material down (−8.20)."}],"opening":{"name":"Caro-Kann Defense","eco":"B10","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 5.g3: 9 moves in all, 5 by White and 4 by Black.","Theory runs out after 5.g3: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}